WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.416
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.416 --> 00:00:03.599
[WHISTLE]

00:00:03.599 --> 00:00:05.140
JONATHAN KOLA: Hello,
Mr. Kubernetes.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:05.800
ELIAS PINTO: Oh, hi there.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.250
JONATHAN KOLA: I have a new
pod version for you to deploy.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.208
ELIAS PINTO: Oh, um, just
leave it right there.

00:00:10.208 --> 00:00:13.260
I need to rebalance this network
traffic between these two pods.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:14.160
JONATHAN KOLA: Wow,
it looks like you have

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.080
your hands full over there.

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:16.530
ELIAS PINTO: Just
trying to do my job.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.571
It would be really nice
to get some help with all

00:00:18.571 --> 00:00:19.571
of this network traffic.

00:00:19.571 --> 00:00:20.779
JONATHAN KOLA: You know what?

00:00:20.779 --> 00:00:22.150
I think I have just the thing.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:23.700
It's called Istio.

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:26.668
ELIAS PINTO: Can you
please show it to me?

00:00:26.668 --> 00:00:29.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.220
JONATHAN KOLA: Istio helps
you manage and understand

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.080
the interactions and
network communication

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.510
between microservices
in your environment.

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:41.190
It is completely
open source, can

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:43.694
be deployed in and across
multiple environments,

00:00:43.694 --> 00:00:46.110
whether you're working with
virtual machines or containers

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:47.827
with an orchestrator
like Kubernetes,

00:00:47.827 --> 00:00:49.410
and consists of a
number of components

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.035
that when deployed
in your environment,

00:00:51.035 --> 00:00:53.670
transparently layer on top of
your distributed application

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:56.190
to allow you to do things like
monitoring and load balancing

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:57.995
without any code changes.

00:00:57.995 --> 00:00:59.370
In this short
walk-through, we'll

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:02.010
see how we can use Istio to
do very fine-grained traffic

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:04.470
routing in a distributed
application deployed

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:05.431
on Kubernetes.

00:01:05.431 --> 00:01:07.180
This becomes useful
when deploying updates

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:09.220
to your application with
patterns like Canary

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:11.100
or blue-green deployments.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.484
I have a cluster running on
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:01:13.484 --> 00:01:15.150
And I've already
configured it to ensure

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.194
that any pod I deploy into
my cluster uses Istio.

00:01:18.194 --> 00:01:20.110
So I'm going to be able
to monitor and control

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:22.740
the traffic to and from these
pods for free without any code

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.090
changes.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.410
Currently running
on this cluster,

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:30.420
I have a very simple application
that consists of two services.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:32.870
The first service
is the UI service.

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:35.490
This service is responsible
for rendering and serving

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:38.810
the user interface upon
request from the outside world.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:41.190
I have another service
called the color service,

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:44.850
which specifies the color
of particular UI components.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.400
So before the UI service can
render and serve the page,

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.080
it has to consult
the color service

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.090
to know what colors to use.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:54.570
Taking a look at
the pods, we should

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:57.810
see that I only have one
pod serving the UI service

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:00.900
and four pods serving
the color service.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.480
Now suppose you want to roll
out a newer version of the color

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:05.670
service, but you don't
want to go all in,

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.470
but want to roll it out
gradually in controlled stages.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:10.870
For example, here I have a
second version of my color

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:12.300
service that I want to deploy.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.370
You could use Kubernetes
deployments for that.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:15.360
You create another deployment.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.130
You label it
appropriately, so that it's

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:19.230
included in the same
load balanced group

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:20.350
as the v1 service.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:23.220
Then gradually change the ratio
of v2 pods to v1 pods to route

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.780
the desired amount of
traffic to either version.

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:28.570
So let's go ahead and
deploy that v2 service.

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:32.110
And we can verify that it
was deployed successfully.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:36.120
And here, we can see we have
one v2 pod and four v1 pods.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:37.950
Now when I go to
my application, I

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.170
see my UI is displaying
a blue button.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.050
The color is coming
from a v1 service.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:44.020
But as I refresh the
button multiple times,

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:45.910
I see a green
button on occasion,

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:47.740
which is coming from
my v2 color service.

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:50.520
And this happens about
roughly 20% of the time.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.020
Now, the limitation
of this approach

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.780
is that the only way to control
the percentage of traffic that

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:57.240
goes to either pod is
by directly scaling

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.110
each version up or down.

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:01.800
So if you only want 10%
traffic to hit your v2 pod,

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.080
you have to have nine
v1 pods and one v2 pod.

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:07.560
Istio allows us to decouple
this and shift traffic

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.930
from one version to another
independently of the number

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:12.110
of replicas for either service.

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:14.610
Istio introduces a resource
called a virtual service, which

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:15.840
allows me to do that.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.240
To deploy a virtual
service, I specify

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.220
how much traffic I want
to go to each service

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:22.803
and the virtual service resource
will ensure these proportions

00:03:22.803 --> 00:03:25.290
are honored irrespective of
how many pods of each service

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:26.659
is actually present.

00:03:26.659 --> 00:03:28.950
So taking a look here, I have
a virtual service defined

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:31.620
that is configured to
send 100% of traffic

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:35.370
to my v1 pod and none
to the v2 service.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.170
Now, we can deploy
this configuration

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:39.000
and actually test
that this is the case.

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:45.860
And when I switch
to my application,

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:49.720
I can see that no matter how
many times I refresh, I always

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:50.860
get served the blue button.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:53.740
That's my v1 service.

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:55.940
Now if I go back to
my configuration,

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:57.130
I can switch this.

00:03:57.130 --> 00:04:01.150
I can route 90% of my
traffic to my v1 service

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:04.310
and 10% of the traffic
to the v2 service.

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:08.950
And if I deploy this again,
we should see a predictable

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:13.540
result. Roughly 10% of requests
will get served a green button,

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:14.680
and the rest-- the 90%--

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:17.890
will get served a blue button.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:22.780
Taking this further, I can
switch the weights to 50/50--

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:24.640
so 50% of traffic
to the v1 service,

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:27.020
50% of traffic to
the v2 service--

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:29.440
and deploy this change.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:31.750
And if I go back
to the application,

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:34.450
I should see roughly
every other time,

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:36.610
the button is either
blue or green.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:39.580
And then finally, I can
route 100% of my traffic

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:43.360
to the v2 service and 0% of
traffic to the v1 service.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:46.030
Apply that change, and if I
go back to my application,

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:49.570
I should always get
served the green button.

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:51.040
And going back to
our deployments

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:53.360
just to verify that the
ratios are still the same,

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:57.610
we should still see only
one v2 pod and four v1 pods.

00:04:57.610 --> 00:05:00.490
So using Istio, you really
get a deep level of control

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:03.515
over how your traffic is routed
across your microservices.

00:05:03.515 --> 00:05:04.810
Hey, Mr. Kubernetes.

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:06.105
You're looking a little better.

00:05:06.105 --> 00:05:07.480
ELIAS PINTO: Thank
you, Jonathan,

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:09.370
so much for showing me Istio.

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:12.430
I now spend my day making
sure my services are up

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:14.504
and I don't have to play
traffic cop anymore.

00:05:14.504 --> 00:05:15.670
JONATHAN KOLA: That's right.

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:17.961
Using Istio gives you a lot
of control over the traffic

00:05:17.961 --> 00:05:19.390
with a lot more ease.

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:20.710
ELIAS PINTO: Exactly.

